{"name":"Dried Edamame","hex":"#b19f80","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"dried","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Without water or moisture, said of something that has previously been wet or moist; resulting from the process of drying.","Usually of foods: cured, preserved by drying."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["simple past and past participle of dry"]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/dried"},{"term":"edamame","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Fresh green soybeans boiled as a vegetable."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/edamame"}],"entries":[{"title":"Drying","blocks":[{"text":"Drying is a mass transfer process consisting of the removal of water or another solvent by evaporation from a solid, semi-solid or liquid. This process is often used as a final production step before selling or packaging products. To be considered \"dried\", the final product must be solid, in the form of a continuous sheet, long pieces, particles or powder. A source of heat and an agent to remove the vapor produced by the process are often involved. In bioproducts like food, grains, and pharmaceuticals like vaccines, the solvent to be removed is almost invariably water. Desiccation may be synonymous with drying or considered an extreme form of drying."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/36/Raumluft_waeschetrockner.jpg/330px-Raumluft_waeschetrockner.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Drying"},{"title":"Edamame","blocks":[{"text":"Edamame  is an East Asian and Southeast Asia dish prepared with immature soybeans in their pods, which are boiled or steamed, and may be served with salt or other condiments. It is consumed in many world regions. Edamame is a common side dish in Chinese cuisine, Japanese cuisine, Filipino cuisine and as an appetizer to alcoholic beverages, such as beer or shōchū. As an ingredient, edamame is used in both sweet and savory dishes, such as takikomi gohan, tempura, and zunda-mochi. Edamame is called utaw in Filipino and maudou in Chinese."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e7/Edamame_by_Zesmerelda_in_Chicago.jpg/330px-Edamame_by_Zesmerelda_in_Chicago.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Edamame"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/36/Stockfish_in_Lofoten.jpg/500px-Stockfish_in_Lofoten.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/36/Stockfish_in_Lofoten.jpg/1280px-Stockfish_in_Lofoten.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/36/Stockfish_in_Lofoten.jpg/500px-Stockfish_in_Lofoten.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Drying of fish in Lofoten in the production of stockfish","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AStockfish_in_Lofoten.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/cc/%E5%A1%A9%E6%8C%AF%E3%82%8A_%2838467083232%29.jpg/500px-%E5%A1%A9%E6%8C%AF%E3%82%8A_%2838467083232%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/cc/%E5%A1%A9%E6%8C%AF%E3%82%8A_%2838467083232%29.jpg/1280px-%E5%A1%A9%E6%8C%AF%E3%82%8A_%2838467083232%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/cc/%E5%A1%A9%E6%8C%AF%E3%82%8A_%2838467083232%29.jpg/500px-%E5%A1%A9%E6%8C%AF%E3%82%8A_%2838467083232%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Edamame and shōchū","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3A%E5%A1%A9%E6%8C%AF%E3%82%8A_(38467083232).jpg"},